---
import Base from "../../../layouts/Base.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import ComponentPreview from "../../../components/ComponentPreview.astro";
import { SliderDemo } from "../../../components/react-demos/slider";

const install = `pnpm add @skryensya/core @skryensya/vanilla @skryensya/react`;

const html = `<div class="sk-inline">
  <input
    class="sk-slider"
    type="range"
    min="0"
    max="100"
    value="65"
    data-sk-slider
    aria-label="Volumen"
    style="--sk-slider-fill: 0.65"
  />
  <input
    class="sk-slider"
    type="range"
    min="0"
    max="100"
    value="30"
    disabled
    aria-label="Brightness (disabled)"
    style="--sk-slider-fill: 0.3"
  />
</div>`;

const react = `import { Slider } from "@skryensya/react/slider";

function Example() {
  return (
    <div className="sk-inline">
      <Slider
        aria-label="Volumen"
        defaultValue={65}
        onValueChange={SomeFunction}
      />
      <Slider aria-label="Brillo" defaultValue={30} disabled />
    </div>
  );
}`;
---

<Base title="Slider" description="Slider: native range with skin, painted fill without JavaScript and React component.">
  <h1>Slider</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Slider is a real <code>&lt;input type="range"&gt;</code> with skin: keyboard, form participation and accessibility tree come from the platform. The filled portion is exposed as the <code>--sk-slider-fill</code> (0–1) property: the initial value is painted by the CSS itself, and following your thumb while dragging is all the binding needs.
  </p>

  <ComponentPreview html={html} react={react} label="Slider">
    <Fragment slot="react">
      <SliderDemo client:load />
    </Fragment>
  </ComponentPreview>
  <p>In Vanilla, <code>data-sk-slider</code> records the minimum binding that rewrites <code>--sk-slider-fill</code> in each <code>input</code>, nothing more: keyboard, focus and form remain platform-specific. In React, <code>&lt;Slider&gt;</code> keeps the property in sync in controlled and uncontrolled mode, and reports the change as a number via <code>onValueChange</code>.</p>

  <h2>Install</h2>
  <CodeBlock code={install} label="terminal" />

  <h2>React</h2>
  <p>The code is in the <strong>React</strong> tab of the preview.</p>
</Base>
